## Why This Is Asked

Introduces a concrete, beginner-friendly scenario around bursty real-time workloads and backpressure, which differs from prior questions by focusing on a push-notification path with skew and feature-flag impact.

## Key Concepts

- Bursty load modeling and ramp patterns
- Tail latency (p99) and error rate targets
- Backpressure mechanisms (token bucket, ingress shaping)
- Observability: queue depth, broker latency, downstream saturation
- Feature flag experiments for performance impact

## Code Example

```javascript
import http from 'k6/http';
import { sleep } from 'k6';
export const options = { thresholds: { 'http_req_duration': ['p99<250'] } };
export default function () {
  http.post('https://api.example.com/notify', JSON.stringify({user:'u1'}), { headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' } });
  sleep(0.01);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure backpressure effects if the broker queues grow beyond capacity?
- How would you validate that feature-flag changes do not degrade latency under bursty load?

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical understanding of GraphQL-specific performance issues, notably nested resolvers and potential N+1 queries, in a beginner-friendly, repeatable way.

## Key Concepts
- GraphQL resolver timings
- N+1 query patterns and detection
- Field-level instrumentation (Apollo tracing)
- Load testing patterns: ramp and spike
- Layer isolation with mocks/replayed traces

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: k6 load test for GraphQL
import http from 'k6/http';
import { check, sleep } from 'k6';
export default function () {
  const payload = JSON.stringify({ query: "{ user(id:1){ id name posts { id title } } }" });
  const res = http.post('https://api.example.com/graphql', payload, { headers: { 'Content-Type': 'application/json' } });
  check(res, { 'status is 200': (r) => r.status === 200 });
  sleep(1);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you detect N+1 without changing application code?
- What instrumentation would you add if you could only modify test data?

